e modulo $3$, is $4\\sim10$?","options":["Yes, because $4$ and $10$ have the same remainder modulo $3$","No, because the sum $4+10=14$ is not divisible by $3$","No, because the difference $10-4=6$ does not equal $3$","Yes, because $4$ and $10$ are both even, hence congruent modulo $3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"86f18f0a8ee4c30d511ef5d5","time_limit":35},{"id":"la_batch22_equivalence_relation_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which relation on real numbers is not an equivalence relation?","options":["$a=b$","$a-b$ is an integer","$a \\lt b$","$|a|=|b|$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"33b78d04d92d9527e9ef8f7d","time_limit":20},{"id":"la_batch22_equivalence_relation_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Equal cardinality of sets is an equivalence relation. What proves the symmetric part?","options":["A composition of bijections is a bijection","The identity map on a set is a bijection","The inverse of a bijection is a bijection","Each injection $A\\to B$ gives one $B\\to A$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"75247f0230541e21e6b14281","time_limit":25},{"id":"la_batch22_equivalence_relation_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is symmetry not the same as reflexivity?","options":["Reflexivity says every relation can be reversed","Symmetry is between two objects; reflexivity is an object with itself","The two words describe the same condition, written once for pairs and once for single objects","Symmetry says every object is related to itself"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"abe45e1f38ad786da7f56a6e","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch22_equivalence_relation_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"The relation $a\\le b$ on real numbers fails to be an equivalence relation mainly because which property fails?","options":["Transitivity","The relation is not defined","Symmetry","Reflexivity"],"